WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:05.860
Testing third-party libraries means testing the way the product actually uses them.

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:14.120
Integration tests should exercise the selected version, enabled features, compile settings, and deployment layout.

3
00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:23.900
Boundary tests should cover invalid input, oversized input, unsupported encodings, malformed files, unexpected error returns, and resource limits.

4
00:00:24.000 --> 00:00:28.240
The goal is to verify product behavior at the dependency boundary.

5
00:00:28.520 --> 00:00:36.920
Sanitizer builds can reveal memory, lifetime, undefined behavior, threading, and leak issues in integration paths.

6
00:00:37.020 --> 00:00:42.700
Defensive fuzzing can also help harden parsers, decoders, and conversion layers at a high level.

7
00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:53.920
The point is practical validation, not misuse guidance; varied inputs help wrappers, error handling, and cleanup paths get exercised before users depend on them.

8
00:00:54.200 --> 00:00:59.880
Runtime defenses may reduce impact when a dependency handles complex or untrusted data.

9
00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:13.020
Sandboxing, least privilege, process isolation, file access limits, resource quotas, timeout controls, and constrained worker processes can all be useful depending on the architecture.

10
00:01:13.120 --> 00:01:20.160
These defenses do not replace updates or good integration, but they can reduce the consequences of a dependency failure.

11
00:01:20.440 --> 00:01:28.840
Review should cover purpose, ownership, configuration, API usage, error handling, update plan, and removal strategy.

12
00:01:28.940 --> 00:01:34.140
A dependency change can alter the product as much as an application source change.

13
00:01:34.240 --> 00:01:40.800
Treating findings as tracked engineering defects keeps the work visible and helps the team improve its dependency process over time.
